  "note": "Paper-grounded component JSON corpus for epic #12 issue #98: representative components used by chat / title / player-info / scoreboard paths. Each `input` is the exact component JSON a packet carries (stored as a JSON string so the bytes fed to the Paper oracle equal the bytes the Rust side parses). `accept` records Paper's actual verdict in this oracle context (Bootstrap-only, no Holder resolution) and matches golden.json. `malformed-*` entries are strict malformed fixtures Paper rejects. The `hover_*`/`click_*` entries are the corrected Paper-accepted fixtures (issue #98): their field names are exactly Paper 26.2's codec schemas - ShowText `value`, OpenUrl `url`, RunCommand `command`, CopyToClipboard `value` - and none needs registry/Holder context (show_text is a plain component, open_url a URI, run_command/copy_to_clipboard plain strings), so Paper accepts all four and the only reason Rivet rejects them is the unported `ClickEvent`/`HoverEvent` codecs (STUB, epic #12): a documented parity divergence until the STUB is ported. The four `malformed-*-wrong-key` fixtures are the same content with the wrong field name (show_text `contents`, open_url `href`, run_command `value`, copy_to_clipboard `text`): Paper rejects them, pinning the field names as load-bearing and keeping the STUB-divergence attribution honest - the corrected entries fail in Rust only at the real codec/STUB boundary, never for registry/Holder context or malformed fields. Numeric-format cases here are the translatable numeric args the chat JSON codec supports (#92 numbers + translation are not on main yet; StyledFormat/FixedFormat are NBT-persistence scoreboard shapes outside ComponentSerialization.CODEC).",
